          "status" : "SUCCESS",
          "results" : [
            {
              "message" : "<h1 id=\"demo-2b-where-trees-cool-most--geographically-weighted-regression-gwr-with-spatial-ai-oml4py\">Demo 2b: Where Trees Cool Most – Geographically Weighted Regression (GWR) with Spatial AI (OML4Py)</h1>\n<p><strong>HrOUG 2026 · Spatial Meets AI</strong> · Which ZET stops need shade first?</p>\n<p>The LISA notebook showed where Zagreb is hot. This notebook asks what drives the heat, and whether the answer is the same everywhere: <strong>spatial heterogeneity</strong>. One global regression gives one effect of tree canopy for the whole city. A geographically weighted regression (GWR) fits a local regression around every H3 cell, so the effect of trees can differ from one part of the city to another.</p>\n<p>It answers three questions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>How much do trees cool on average?</strong> A global OLS regression of surface temperature on land cover, with spatial diagnostics.</li>\n<li><strong>Do trees cool the same everywhere?</strong> GWR: the local cooling effect of tree canopy for every H3 cell, on a map.</li>\n<li><strong>What if?</strong> 15 percentage points of tree canopy instead of built-up area at a stop: how much cooler does its cell get, at stops in different parts of the city?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Adapted from the Oracle sample notebook <em>OML4Py Spatial AI GWR Regressor</em> (Copyright (c) 2025 Oracle Corporation, <a rel=\"nofollow\" href=\"https://oss.oracle.com/licenses/upl/\">The Universal Permissive License (UPL), Version 1.0</a>).</p>\n",
              "type" : "HTML"
            }
          ],
          "forms" : "[]"
        },
