        "message" : [
          "%md",
          "",
          "###### Prepare land cover per H3 cell",
          "",
          "The explanatory variables come from [ESA WorldCover 2021](https://esa-worldcover.org) (10 m land cover classes), grouped per H3 cell:",
          "",
          "| Variable | WorldCover classes |",
          "|---|---|",
          "| `CANOPY_PCT` | 10 tree cover |",
          "| `GREEN_PCT` | 20 shrubland, 30 grassland, 40 cropland, 90 herbaceous wetland, 95 mangroves, 100 moss and lichen |",
          "| `WATER_PCT` | 80 permanent water bodies: stored, but not a model variable (see *2. Do trees cool the same everywhere?*) |",
          "| `BUILT_PCT` | 50 built-up, 60 bare or sparse vegetation: the reference category (together with water), not a model variable |",
          "",
          "1. The database downloads the land cover for the extent of the surface temperature raster: 3 × 3 cells of 10 m per 30 m pixel, as a NumPy array.",
          "2. Python computes the share of each group per 30 m pixel and averages it per H3 cell, over the valid pixels in `ZG_LST_PIXELS`. Surface temperature and land cover use the same pixels.",
          "3. SQL creates `ZG_H3_FEATURES`.",
          "",
          "The land cover map is from 2021, the surface temperature from 2026; changes in between are not captured.",
          "",
          "Run this section once. The analysis starts at *1. How much do trees cool on average?*"
        ],
        "selectedVisualization" : "html",
